      "id": 1,
      "text": "Green Valley’s site was bought from the government in 1852 for $3 an acre by Samuel Schureman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://pekinpubliclibrary.org/green-valleys-long-and-fertile-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Illinois Central Railroad arrived in 1870, and a grain elevator followed two years later, anchoring the town’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://pekinpubliclibrary.org/green-valleys-long-and-fertile-history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A local paper, the Green Valley Banner, ran from the late 1890s until the Great Depression cut it short in the 1930s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://pekinpubliclibrary.org/green-valleys-long-and-fertile-history/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1922 Sanborn Fire Insurance Map is the only surviving detailed map of Green Valley, now available in print.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
